From 0e0122eedd168ef80e89a494e9946b893771ba22 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 09:27:04 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] drm: Choose a frame sequential frame's eye from the refresh
 it targets

KWin's software frame sequential twins chose the eye of a commit for
the refresh right after the last completed one. When a page-flip event
is handled after the next refresh has already passed, the render loop
targets the refresh after that one, and the frame landed there with
the other eye: the refresh that passed showed the wrong eye, and so did
the one the frame landed on, before the order relocked.

The scheduler now also keeps the timestamp of the last completion, and
a frame gets the eye of the refresh its targetPageflipTime is for,
counted in refreshes of the mode from that completion. Whether a new
pair starts is decided the same way, from the render loop's next
presentation, which is the target of the frame being prepared. A
commit without a frame, such as a modeset, and a completion without a
timestamp keep the eye after the last completed refresh.

testMockDrmFrameSequential::testEyeOfTargetedRefresh hands the
pipeline a completion on refresh 100 a refresh and a quarter late; the
next frame targets refresh 102 and must show the left eye and start a
new pair, then relock on time with the right eye for 103.
frameSequentialTest covers the scheduler's arithmetic.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5
